Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "opponent next-possession points per possession after offensive play type X",
    "AVOID labelling from suppression trigger and counter recommendations",
    "own-team possession sequence lags after play type X",
    "play-type label reliability",
    "data quality of rule-based play-type classifier"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to measure opponent next-possession PPP per play type with adequate sample size?",
    "What confounding (game state, lineup, opponent defensive quality) must be controlled when linking play type to opponent's subsequent possession?",
    "Is there lookahead bias if the AVOID label and the lagged PPP come from the same game?",
    "Can label noise in play-type assignment create spurious correlations with opponent PPP?",
    "What multiplicity adjustment is needed for testing many play types and lags?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-050",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Opponent play type on the preceding possession may suppress a star's next-possession PPP (PUTBACK is top suppressor for 6+ stars), but sample sizes are 32-95 for many cells, no multiplicity correction, and the analysis was not a formal hypothesis test."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Play-type PPP differences are artefacts of label/foul leakage: OTHER PPP inflated by 36% foul endings, DRIVE PPP 12 points below foul benchmark, and only 0.7% of possessions map to a real play type via initiation_type."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Inferred defensive scheme changes from play-type labels cannot separate real adjustment from offense execution; near-uniform transition matrices are consistent with label noise."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-074",
      "status_as_recorded": "rejected",
      "finding": "No elite creator produces a positive lineup-matched teammate-PPP lift on the next 1-3 possessions (powered null). A secondary structural initiator effect survived for Antetokounmpo but not efficiency."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
      "what_failed": "using play-type labelled possessions to compute PPP differences",
      "why": "labeling artifact: foul endings inflate OTHER PPP and leak from DRIVE; 0.7% initiation_type mapping indicates the labels do not reflect true play initiation."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "what_failed": "inferring defensive scheme changes from within-game play-type mix",
      "why": "label/classification error; cannot disentangle offensive execution from defensive adjustment; near-uniform transition suggests noise."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-050",
      "what_failed": "characterising opponent play type suppression of a star",
      "why": "small sample sizes (n_after 32-95) and no multiplicity correction made the results unreliable."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Existing play-type labels contain substantial artefacts (foul leakage, misclassification) that distort PPP metrics.",
    "Inferred play-type labels are noisy; changes in observed play-type mix often cannot be attributed to genuine defensive adjustments.",
    "Lagged own-team PPP effects from elite-creator actions are essentially null in a well-powered matched test.",
    "Small-sample analyses of play-type-conditional opponent PPP are subject to high variance and multiplicity issues."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether opponent next-possession PPP after a specific offensive play type is systematically different from average opponent PPP.",
    "Whether such differences, if they exist, are large enough to influence AVOID label assignment.",
    "Whether own-sequence lagged PPP after a play type matters for anything beyond what is already captured by within-game suppression triggers.",
    "That a clean test free of label noise would still yield a null or positive result."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The research question can be operationalised by computing opponent PPP on the possession immediately following an offensive possession of type X, and by computing the offensive team's PPP on its next k possessions after running play X.",
    "Any signal that survives label noise is likely to be small because the effect works through a chain (offensive play type -> end-of-possession event -> opponent transition -> opponent possession) that introduces many confounders.",
    "Given the AVOID labels are derived from within-game PPP changes, using within-game opponent next-possession PPP to predict those labels may create circularity if the same data window is used; a clean design would need to hold out games or use pre-trigger segments only.",
    "The available data (full play-by-play with derived play-type records from 2022-23 to 2025-26) provides enough possessions overall, but power will be low for rarely called play types."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Precise definition of AVOID labels and whether they are deterministic or probabilistic thresholds.",
    "Whether the project can isolate 'direct' (non-inferred) play types from the rule-based classifier to reduce label noise.",
    "The exact handling of possession transitions (turnovers, end-of-quarter, technical free throws) and whether the data link opponent possessions reliably.",
    "The magnitude of an effect that would be considered 'large enough to drive AVOID labels'."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "Without the historical evidence the question might have been viewed as an exploratory correlation. The records show that play-type labels contain systematic artifacts (foul leakage, misclassification) and that lagged PPP effects have been null in a similar setting, which raises the risk of artefactual findings and lowers the expected signal-to-noise ratio. This pushes the recommendation from 'proceed with caution' to 'do not proceed' unless label quality can be improved."
