        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Rare play types will have very few opponent next-possession observations; even common types may yield only hundreds of observations per team per season, limiting power to detect small but practically meaningful differences."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF-APPA-050",
      "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
      "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "FAM:SF-APPS-074"
    ],
    "summary": "No prior study directly examined opponent next-possession PPP after an offensive play type as a driver of AVOID labels. Related work found that play-type labels are artefact-laden, that lagged own-team PPP effects are null, and that opponent-play-type analyses suffer from small samples and multiplicity concerns."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
      "text": "Existing play-type labels have documented foul leakage and misclassification; these can distort both the independent variable (play type X) and the outcome (opponent PPP if the previous possession's ending influences the next possession's context)."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Many play types occur infrequently, leading to wide confidence intervals on opponent next-possession PPP; an 'AVOID' decision based on such unstable estimates would be unreliable."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "The opponent's defensive quality, game score, period, and substitution patterns influence both which play type the offense runs and how the opponent performs on the next possession."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "If the same game is used to compute opponent next-possession PPP and to determine AVOID labels (which depend on within-game PPP changes), information from later possessions can leak into the predictor, inflating the apparent relationship."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Testing many play types and multiple lags simultaneously creates a large multiplicity problem that, if uncorrected, will produce false positives."
    },
    {
      "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
      "text": "Playing styles and the classifier may change across the four available seasons, potentially violating the assumption of stationarity when pooling data."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.1,
    "rejected": 0.3,
    "untestable": 0.3,
    "artifact": 0.2,
    "uncertain": 0.1
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The difference between opponent next-possession PPP when the preceding offensive possession is of play type X and the league-average opponent next-possession PPP on all possessions (adjusted for game state and opponent defensive quality). For own-sequence lags: the change in the offensive team's PPP on possession t+k after a play-type-X possession at t, compared with its typical PPP at that lag.",
    "unit": "a possession (offensive or opponent) linked to a preceding play-type-labelled offensive possession; for lagged own sequence, a possession within the same team's subsequent k possessions.",
    "sample": "All NBA regular-season and playoff games from 2022-23 through 2025-26 (full seasons where available), excluding garbage time (Q4 margin >20, or Q4 with <2 min and >10), and possessions that start with under 3 seconds remaining.",
    "power": "A priori power analysis: for the most frequent play type (e.g., PNR) ~10,000 opponent next-possession observations per season, which could detect a Cohen's d of about 0.03 at 80% power with alpha=0.01 (corrected for 10 play types x 3 lags). Rare play types like PUTBACK (~500 obs) could detect only d~0.12 under the same conditions.",
    "controls": "Fixed effects for offensive team, opponent team, period (1,2,3,4), score margin (binned), and an indicator for whether the preceding offensive possession ended in a defensive rebound vs. a made basket vs. a turnover.",
    "falsification": "1) Replace the preceding play type with a random draw from the offensive team's play-type distribution and show that the relationship with opponent PPP disappears. 2) Test whether opponent next-possession PPP following timeouts or end-of-quarter breaks (no play type) shows similar variance. 3) Use only directly tagged (non-inferred) play types and check if the pattern holds.",
    "multiplicity": "Bonferroni-Holm correction across all play-type–lag combinations; pre-registration of a primary hypothesis for the three most frequent play types and lag 1 only.",
    "decision_rule": "The effect of play type X is considered large enough to influence AVOID if, after adjustment, its opponent next-possession PPP exceeds the league average by at least 0.05 PPP with a one-sided FDR < 0.05, and the effect is stable across seasons and not accounted for by the falsification checks.",
    "data_version": "Per-game files from NBA stats API as of 2026-06-19, with derived play-type possession records from the project's graph database, snapshot of the 2025-26 regular season and playoffs through 2026-04-13 and 2026-06-21 respectively."
  }
}

